<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lê Văn Khôi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 1834</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Le Van Khoi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lê Văn Khôi was a Vietnamese officer, the adopted son of the southern governor Lê Văn Duyệt, who led a revolt against Emperor Minh Mạng in 1833. Supported by Catholics, Chinese settlers and former followers of Duyệt, he seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> citadel and sought help from Siam. He died during the siege in 1834, and the citadel fell in 1835, after which its defenders were executed.</p></div>
